    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d29-a16">

                <pb facs="0592" n="582" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv10/pg582"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Holland House, Bury Street, E. C.</hi> 3</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1919-08-14">14 August,
                        1919</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Messrs. The Royal Commission on
                        Wheat Supplies</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Trafalgar House, Waterloo
                        Place, S. W. 1</hi>.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sirs</hi>: We have received the following letter
                    from Messrs. Wm. H. Muller &amp; Co., the Hague:— <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Our representative in Germany raises the question whether it would not
                            be possible that some of the steamers which discharge German grain in
                            one of the German ports, and which return to the Argentine, take some
                            outward cargo of German goods to South America. As we understand that
                            the blockade has been lifted and Dutch liners are now allowed to accept
                            German outward cargo, it is not absolutely impossible that you come to
                            an agreement with Captain Elliot, or at least that Captain Elliot raises
                            this question before the Economic Council. This, of course, would mean
                            the beginning of the direct export from Germany oversea.</p>
                        <p>We are simply forwarding this proposition to you as we have received it
                            from German side and shall be glad to receive your
                    reply.”</p></quote></p>
                <p>We shall be glad if you will enable us to reply.</p>
                <p>Yours faithfully,</p>
                <closer><hi rendition="#italic">For Wm. H. Muller &amp; Co</hi>. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">London</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">Ltd</hi>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
